Sensitivity: Sensitivity or recall indicates the percentage of samples that have been predicted correctly.
(1)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
}{}$$Sensitivity = {\ }{{TP} \over {TP + FN}}{\ } \times 100$$\end{document}

Specificity: Specificity shows the percentage of negative samples that have been predicted correctly as negative samples.
(2)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
}{}$$Specificity = {\ }{{TN} \over {TN + FP}} \times 100$$\end{document}

Accuracy: Accuracy is a ratio between the correctly classified data points to the total number of samples (138).
(3)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
}{}$$Accuracy = {\ }{{TP + TN} \over {TP + FP + TN + FN}}{\ } \times 100$$\end{document}

